{
  "policy_id": "ref-92-stop-offshore-tax-avoidance-by-care",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Stop offshore tax avoidance by care home providers",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Closing offshore tax loopholes used by care home providers would, if effective, bring more corporation tax into the UK Treasury \u2014 but the sums involved are modest and enforcement against complex offshore structures is historically difficult, so the fiscal gain is uncertain. No independent costing of this specific measure exists.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether HMRC can successfully enforce against the complex offshore structures in practice, given acknowledged difficulties obtaining information from other jurisdictions, determines whether any fiscal improvement materialises at all.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy targets larger care home providers using offshore property company structures and high-interest shareholder loans to avoid UK tax.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "specifically targeting larger providers avoiding tax on profits through complex offshore property company structures and high interest shareholder loans"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Offshore companies own around 12% of care homes run by the four largest providers, with most based in secrecy jurisdictions.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "offshore companies own 82 (12%) of 706 care homes run by the four largest providers in England, with 75 of these ownership companies based in secrecy jurisdictions like Jersey or the Isle of Man"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The 26 largest providers channelled \u00a3117 million in debt repayments to related companies, identified as a tax-avoidance method.",
        "evidence_id": "E3",
        "quote": "The CHPI found that the 26 largest providers alone channeled \u00a3117 million in debt repayments to related companies, a practice identified as a method of avoiding tax and concealing profits"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Structures use tax havens to artificially create UK losses and shift profits offshore.",
        "evidence_id": "E1",
        "quote": "These structures frequently utilize tax havens such as Jersey, Guernsey, Luxembourg, the Isle of Man, and the Cayman Islands to artificially create losses in the UK and shift profits offshore"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "HMRC acknowledges significant challenges enforcing against offshore non-compliance due to structural complexity and information barriers.",
        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "HMRC also acknowledges the significant challenges in tackling offshore tax non-compliance due to the \"complexity of structures used by wealthy individuals\" and difficulties in obtaining information from other jurisdictions"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Legislative anti-avoidance responses historically trigger new schemes, creating an arms race with limited net fiscal gain.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "legislative responses often lead to new schemes"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "OBR costings for anti-avoidance measures have historically underperformed original estimates by 15\u201365%.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "Historically, OBR costings for such measures have often fallen short of original estimates, by amounts varying between 15% and 65% in some categories"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "No independent body has produced a specific fiscal impact estimate for curbing offshore avoidance in the care home sector.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "they have not produced specific reports on the financial impact of curbing offshore tax avoidance in the care home sector or on Reform UK's specific policy"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy addresses a real and documented fiscal leak: care home providers routing profits through offshore structures in secrecy jurisdictions, with \u00a3117m in related-party debt repayments among just the 26 largest providers. If these flows were successfully taxed in the UK, there would be a net gain to public revenues \u2014 meaning less borrowing or more spending capacity \u2014 which is a direct O12 improvement. However, the magnitude is bounded by several constraints. The share of care homes in offshore ownership is around 12% of the largest four providers' portfolios, not the whole sector. More importantly, the evidence base strongly flags enforcement risk: HMRC explicitly acknowledges difficulty obtaining information from other jurisdictions, and the House of Commons Library notes that anti-avoidance legislation routinely triggers new schemes. OBR track record on similar measures shows realised yields 15\u201365% below estimates. The policy text offers no committed enforcement instrument, budget uplift for HMRC, or statutory mechanism beyond stating intent to 'stop' avoidance \u2014 making the deliverable uncertain. No independent fiscal costing of this specific measure exists. On balance, the direction is a plausible modest improvement to public finances (more tax collected), but the magnitude is capped at minor and confidence is low given the enforcement uncertainty and absence of any costing. The counterfactual absent the policy is continued profit-shifting with minimal UK tax yield from these structures.",
